# XSH recvmsg()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Local sockets only. The descriptor-passing assertions use AF_UNIX stream
# pairs with SCM_RIGHTS; the page names the type, and XSH 2.10.17 makes
# AF_UNIX sockets mandatory, but no page states that AF_UNIX sockets carry
# it (Q-149), so those assertions that need the descriptor to arrive are
# disputed and the ones that only need the send to work are not. The
# control-message macros of <sys/socket.h> are checked here too, on plain
# buffers (they are what the receive and send tests use to build and read
# ancillary data). Every blocking call is guarded; a receive that must not
# block is preceded by a poll(). A stream receive may return less than
# asked, so tests that need a count loop until they have it. Not tested:
# MSG_EOR and MSG_OOB in msg_flags (protocol-specific), ECONNRESET,
# ETIMEDOUT, EOPNOTSUPP, and the may-fail errors.

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.001
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() of a 9 byte datagram into three buffers of 3, 4 and 5
           bytes returns 9 with the data in order, the first buffer filled
           before the second is used, and MSG_TRUNC clear in msg_flags.
test       t/001-scatter.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.002
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When a datagram (10 bytes) is longer than the buffers (3 and 3
           bytes), recvmsg() fills them with the first 6 bytes, sets
           MSG_TRUNC in msg_flags and discards the excess (nothing is left
           to receive). The return value is 6 or 10.
note       The page says "return the total length of the message" in one
           sentence and the length of the message in the buffer elsewhere;
           for a truncated datagram it is not clear whether that is the
           bytes stored or the whole message (Q-151), so either is
           accepted.
test       t/002-trunc-flag.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.003
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       msg_flags is not read on input and is set on output: with every
           bit set before the call, a complete receipt of a datagram leaves
           MSG_TRUNC, MSG_CTRUNC and MSG_OOB clear.
test       t/003-flags-output.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.004
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() on an unconnected datagram socket stores the AF_UNIX
           address the datagram came from in msg_name (family AF_UNIX, the
           sender's bound path) and sets msg_namelen to a length covering
           the family and the path, not larger than the buffer given.
test       t/004-source-unix.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.005
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() on a UDP socket stores the sender's IPv4 address in
           msg_name: family AF_INET, the sender's bound loopback address
           and port, with msg_namelen covering them.
test       t/005-source-inet.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.006
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When msg_namelen is smaller than the sender's address (4 bytes
           for a 32 character path), recvmsg() stores no more than 4 bytes
           of it in msg_name, and the message is received in full.
test       t/006-name-truncated-bytes.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.007
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       When the stored address is truncated because msg_namelen was
           too small, msg_namelen on return is the length of the sender's
           address: not smaller than the offset of sun_path plus the path
           length.
note       Defect 1565 changed the description of msg_namelen ("on output
           specifies the length of the source address"), as for recvfrom().
test       t/007-name-truncated-length.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.008
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       msg_name may be a null pointer when the source address is not
           wanted: recvmsg() on a datagram socket with a null msg_name
           returns the datagram.
test       t/008-null-name.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.009
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       On a connected stream socket msg_name and msg_namelen are
           ignored: with msg_name pointing at a buffer the buffer is left
           as it was, and with a null msg_name and a non-zero msg_namelen
           the call still succeeds.
test       t/009-connected-name-ignored.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.010
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#ERRORS/EMSGSIZE
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() with msg_iovlen 0 fails with EMSGSIZE.
test       t/010-iovlen-zero.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.011
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#ERRORS/EMSGSIZE
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() with msg_iovlen one more than {IOV_MAX} fails with
           EMSGSIZE.
test       t/011-iovlen-over-max.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.012
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() with two buffers of SSIZE_MAX bytes each, whose total
           overflows ssize_t, fails with EINVAL.
test       t/012-overflow.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.013
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() on an empty non-blocking socket fails with EAGAIN or
           EWOULDBLOCK.
test       t/013-eagain.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.014
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() with a descriptor that is not open fails with EBADF
           (-1, and a closed descriptor).
test       t/014-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.015
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#ERRORS/ENOTSOCK
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() on a descriptor that is not a socket fails with
           ENOTSOCK.
test       t/015-enotsock.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.016
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#ERRORS/ENOTCONN
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() on a stream socket that was never connected fails with
           ENOTCONN.
test       t/016-enotconn.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.017
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() on a stream socket whose peer has performed an orderly
           shutdown (SHUT_WR), with nothing left to receive, returns 0.
test       t/017-orderly-shutdown.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.018
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() with MSG_PEEK on a stream socket returns data without
           consuming it: after the peek, receiving the 6 bytes sent gives
           all of them from the beginning.
test       t/018-peek.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.019
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       recvmsg() with MSG_WAITALL on a stream socket and buffers of 3
           and 5 bytes waits for the data of two sends (3 bytes, then 5
           bytes a moment later) and returns all 8.
test       t/019-waitall.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.020
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      2001
says       A descriptor of a regular file sent over an AF_UNIX stream socket
           with an SCM_RIGHTS control message is received by recvmsg() as a
           control message with level SOL_SOCKET and type SCM_RIGHTS, and
           the descriptor in it refers to the same file (equal device and
           file serial number) and reads its contents.
note       disputed (Q-149): no page states that an AF_UNIX socket carries
           SCM_RIGHTS or what happens if it does not; the test takes AF_UNIX
           as the socket this is meant for. A setup failure of the send is
           UNRESOLVED; a missing control message is a FAIL, logged as
           INSPECT.
test       t/020-scm-rights-file.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.021
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      2001
says       The read end of a pipe sent over an AF_UNIX stream socket with
           SCM_RIGHTS and received with recvmsg() is a live copy: a byte
           later written to the pipe's write end is read through the
           received descriptor.
note       disputed (Q-149), as for XSH.recvmsg.020.
test       t/021-scm-rights-pipe.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.022
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      2001
says       Two descriptors sent in one SCM_RIGHTS message are received as a
           single control message whose cmsg_len is CMSG_LEN(2 * sizeof
           (int)), with both descriptors valid and in order, and
           CMSG_NXTHDR() after it gives a null pointer.
note       disputed (Q-149), as for XSH.recvmsg.020.
test       t/022-scm-rights-two.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.023
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When the control buffer is too small (msg_controllen 0) for the
           SCM_RIGHTS message sent with a data byte, recvmsg() still returns
           the data byte and sets MSG_CTRUNC in msg_flags.
note       If the send cannot be done the test is UNRESOLVED (see
           XSH.recvmsg.020).
test       t/023-ctrunc.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.024
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC, a descriptor received in an SCM_RIGHTS
           message has FD_CLOEXEC set; if no descriptor is delivered the
           test is UNRESOLVED.
test       t/024-cmsg-cloexec.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.025
anchor     functions/recvmsg.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With MSG_CMSG_CLOFORK, a descriptor received in an SCM_RIGHTS
           message has FD_CLOFORK set; if no descriptor is delivered the
           test is UNRESOLVED.
test       t/025-cmsg-clofork.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.026
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       CMSG_LEN(n) is not smaller than the offset of CMSG_DATA() within
           a control message plus n, that offset is not smaller than the
           size of struct cmsghdr, and CMSG_SPACE(n) is not smaller than
           CMSG_LEN(n), for several n from 0 to 100.
note       CMSG_LEN() is the value to store in cmsg_len, which counts the
           header and the data; the data array follows the structure.
test       t/026-cmsg-len-space.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.027
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       CMSG_FIRSTHDR() returns a null pointer when msg_controllen is 0
           and when it is non-zero but smaller than struct cmsghdr, and
           returns msg_control when msg_controllen is exactly the size of
           struct cmsghdr and when it is CMSG_SPACE of a small length.
test       t/027-cmsg-firsthdr.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.028
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       In a zeroed control buffer of CMSG_SPACE(4) + CMSG_SPACE(8)
           bytes holding a message of length 4 first, CMSG_NXTHDR() gives a
           pointer beyond the first message and inside the buffer, and a
           null pointer after the second message of length 8; with a null
           second argument it gives what CMSG_FIRSTHDR() gives.
test       t/028-cmsg-nxthdr.c

assertion  XSH.recvmsg.029
anchor     basedefs/sys_socket.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       CMSG_LEN() and CMSG_SPACE() with an integer constant argument are
           integer constant expressions: they work as array dimensions,
           enumeration values and in a compile-time relation, and the values
           are positive.
test       t/029-cmsg-constexpr.c
